From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AML: MVC Blu-ray subtitle depth, frame by frame

A Blu-ray 3D gives its subtitles a depth for every frame ("1 plane +
offset"): the first access unit of each GOP carries, in an SEI
user_data_unregistered message (OFMD), one signed offset per displayed
frame for each offset sequence, and a PG stream names the sequence it
follows (ss_offset_sequence_id, or a remux's 3d-plane tag, which the
player already passes on as DemuxPacket::subtitlePlane). Kodi read none
of it, and the depth a picture could carry never reached the renderer.
The layout and the missing links are from cinemaONE's CoreELEC/xbmc#77.

The AML codec reads the table from the access unit before Convert(),
keeps the last few GOPs' tables, each from its GOP's first picture in
display order (the access unit that carried it, or an open GOP's leading
picture fed after it), and gives each output picture the offset of its
own pts. The decoder holds several pictures between input and output, so
counting output pictures from the table's arrival would change depth
before the cut it belongs to. A seek drops the tables; a picture past
the end of a table keeps its last offset.

Shared code: a PG overlay is marked as such; the overlay renderer gives
its cached texture the overlay's depth and kind every frame, the overlays
of a group get the picture's depth as a single overlay does. The depth
adds to the subtitle depth setting, as upstream's code already meant.

The OFMD table was only looked for in a top-level user_data_unregistered
message, but MVC streams carry it nested in an MVC scalable nesting SEI
(payload type 37), so no Blu-ray subtitle ever got a depth. Every SEI
message's payload is now searched for the OFMD uuid, a top-level type 5 first,
and the table is bounded by the end of the payload it was found in.

The depth was written onto the overlay objects in the video thread
(CVideoPlayerVideo::ProcessOverlays), objects the overlay renderer already
holds for earlier render buffers and reads on the render thread: the depth of
a later picture took effect several frames early, and the write was not
synchronised. The depth now goes with the render buffer: CRenderManager::
AddOverlay takes it with the overlay and the overlay renderer keeps it in the
buffer's element, and the overlay is never written. Shared code: an optional
argument to AddOverlay in the render manager and the overlay renderer, the
element's field, and the picture's depth made an optional (see below); the
upstream write onto the overlay is removed.

The disc's depth replaces the setting; the setting covers subtitles without
it. A PG subtitle whose picture has an offset (a table was found and the
subtitle's sequence is in it) takes only that offset, in frame packing as in
HFR VRR; other subtitles, PG on discs and remuxes without a table, and PG
before the first table take subtitles.stereoscopicdepth, as upstream. For
that the picture's depth is a std::optional, empty when the stream gives
none, and so is the overlay's.

The offsets are in pixels of a 1920 wide picture and were added in GUI
coordinates: the disc's offset is now scaled by the GUI's width over 1920
(the setting is in GUI pixels and is left as it is).

A packet without a pts was keyed by its dts, which puts a reordered picture
at another place than it is shown: such packets now feed neither a table nor
the GOP start.

The tables are read from a disc's MVC (AMVC, Annex B) as from a remux's
(MVC1): the disc's was left out, so a Blu-ray played from its playlist had
no table and its subtitles took the depth setting.

One INFO line per film says what was found: the first table (sequences x
frames, and the sequence the subtitle follows), or that after 10 s of
pictures the stream carries none; and once if the subtitle's sequence is not
in the table.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
